Article 6 The State shall take the following measures to maintain peace and stability in the Taiwan Strait region and to develop cross-strait relations:
(1) encouraging and facilitating personnel exchanges across the strait to enhance mutual understanding and mutual trust;
(2) encouraging and facilitating economic exchanges and cooperation, realizing direct links in postal, transport, and trade services, and strengthening cross-strait economic ties to achieve mutual benefit and mutual gain;
(3) encouraging and facilitating exchanges in education, science and technology, culture, health, and sports, and jointly promoting the fine traditions of Chinese culture;
(4) encouraging and facilitating joint efforts to combat crime; and
(5) encouraging and facilitating other activities that are conducive to maintaining peace and stability in the Taiwan Strait region and developing cross-strait relations.
The State shall, according to law, protect the rights and interests of the compatriots in Taiwan.

Article 7 The State advocates achieving peaceful reunification through consultations and negotiations on an equal footing between the two sides of the Taiwan Strait. These consultations and negotiations may be conducted step by step and in stages, and the means may be flexible and varied.
The two sides of the Taiwan Strait may consult and negotiate on the following matters:
(1) formally ending the state of hostility between the two sides;
(2) planning for the development of cross-strait relations;
(3) the steps and arrangements for peaceful reunification;
(4) the political status of the Taiwan authorities;
(5) the space for international activities that is commensurate with the status of the Taiwan region; and
(6) any other issues related to the realization of peaceful reunification.
